  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Dyspepsia on naproxen (R10.13)</content>. Dyspepsia from the NSAID, without alarm features; hemoglobin 14.4 g/dL, unchanged from intake, so no sign of bleeding. Under the ACG and CAG guideline he does not need endoscopy at his age without alarm features; testing for H. pylori is advised. [S]<list><item>Stop naproxen.</item><item>Omeprazole 20 mg daily before breakfast for four weeks.</item><item>H. pylori stool antigen test.</item><item>Return at once for black stools, vomiting blood or worsening pain.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Post-traumatic osteoarthritis of the left hindfoot (M19.172)</content>. Much better two weeks after the subtalar injection. With no oral NSAID, diclofenac gel continues (little reaches the bloodstream), with acetaminophen when needed; still no opioids. [S]<list><item>Continue diclofenac gel four times daily.</item><item>Acetaminophen 500 to 1,000 mg up to every 6 hours as needed, no more than 3,000 mg a day.</item><item>Continue the brace and home exercises.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); Indian Health Manual, Part 3, Chapter 30: Chronic Non-Cancer Pain Management (2026); ACG and CAG Clinical Guideline: Management of Dyspepsia (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Phone call with the H. pylori result. Ten-week review in three weeks. Return sooner as above. Next scheduled visit 1886-09-14: ten-week review after the injection: pain, function, surgery discussed and declined for now. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
